  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 141 AM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nWhile the Canadian shortwave dives southeastward, the broader\nlongwave trough it is embedded within will gradually translate \neastward, where a secondary upper-level circulation is progged \nto develop Tuesday night. This secondary feature is forecast to \nremain well to the south of the area through the short-term \nperiod; however, by the beginning of the extended period, \nmoisture advection and increasing PoP will be introduced as this\ncirculation phases and lifts northward. Based on forecast \nsoundings and critical thickness values, a rain-snow mix is \nlikely at the onset as the precipitation shield spreads north \ninto the CWA. As temperatures fall along the diurnal curve and \ncold air advection wraps into the system, precipitation will \ntransition to snow showers overnight Wednesday into \nThanksgiving. As the surface low meanders into Kentucky, the \ndeformation zone on the northwest flank of the departing low \nwill linger over the CWA, maintaining persistent snow showers \nthroughout Thursday morning before diurnal warming allows snow \nshowers to mix with or change back over to rain showers Thursday\nafternoon. As the system exits the region and becomes a \nnor'easter along the Atlantic seaboard, surface high pressure \nwill build back into the area. Given the considerable lead time \nbefore this feature is expected to impact the region, \nsignificant adjustments remain possible in upcoming model \nsuites; therefore, it is prudent to focus on overall forecast \ntrends rather than relying on any single deterministic run or \nmodel suite. Relatively warmer temperatures are progged to build\ninto the region heading into the weekend, though deterministic \nmodels indicate another potential system tracking through the \narea and bringing rain during the latter half of the weekend. \nThis progressive pattern is forecast to repeat itself again from\nMonday into Tuesday. Overall, a storm system will move into the\nregion starting Wednesday night, bringing a rain-snow mix that \ntransitions to snow showers overnight and continues through \nThanksgiving morning before changing back to rain Thursday \nafternoon. Drier and slightly warmer weather returns briefly for\nthe start of the weekend before another round of rain arrives \nlate in the weekend and again early next week.",
